/**
 * FMLA and disability paperwork — the adult counterpart to the school physicals guide.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS
 * Same play: target the job, not the tool. "AI form filler" has no search volume because
 * the category does not exist yet; "FMLA paperwork" is what a physician actually types.
 * When form completion ships, this page is already ranking and only the closing section
 * changes. Do not pre-announce the feature here.
 *
 * THE SPINE IS THE FREQUENCY AND DURATION ESTIMATE, NOT FMLA LAW.
 * An earlier draft tried to be an FMLA reference. That was the wrong page for two
 * reasons: the regulatory thresholds in 29 CFR 825.115 could not be verified from the
 * DOL fact sheet during drafting, and an HR site will always out-rank us on the law.
 * What we can say that nobody else can is clinical: the hardest field on the form is the
 * intermittent-leave estimate, the only defensible basis for it is the patient's own
 * history of flares, and that history is the thing practices cannot retrieve. That is
 * the adult version of the school-form retrieval ratio.
 *
 * ACCURACY BAR, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
 * No numeric thresholds are stated — not days of incapacity, visit counts, or time
 * windows. They exist, they are in the regulation, and they are linked rather than
 * paraphrased. Getting one wrong on a page aimed at physicians completing a legal
 * document is a worse failure than being less specific. The six categories are named
 * because the DOL fact sheet names them.
 *
 * Disability paperwork is mentioned as the same shape but not detailed, because every
 * carrier uses its own form and a generic page about them would say nothing useful.
 */
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can a practice charge a fee for completing FMLA paperwork?',
    a: 'Generally yes. Certification is paperwork for an employer, not a covered medical service, and many practices charge a flat form fee. It is worth having one written policy rather than deciding per request, and telling patients the fee before the form arrives. Check your payer contracts first — a few restrict what you can charge an enrolled patient.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What makes the intermittent-leave section so hard to complete?',
    a: 'It asks for a prediction: how often the employee will be unable to work, and for how long each time. There is no clinical finding that answers it. The only defensible basis is the patient’s own record — how many flares in the past year, how long each lasted, how many were severe enough to stop them working. Most practices cannot retrieve that quickly, so the estimate gets guessed, and a guess is what gets challenged.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What counts as a serious health condition under FMLA?',
    a: 'The Department of Labor defines it as a condition involving either inpatient care or continuing treatment by a health care provider, across six categories including chronic conditions and conditions requiring multiple treatments. The specific thresholds — days of incapacity, visit counts, time windows — are set out in the regulation and are revised from time to time, so check the current text rather than relying on a summary, including this one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much of the form can come from the existing chart?',
    a: 'The diagnosis, the date of onset, the treatment regimen, the visit history and the pattern of past episodes are all already documented. What genuinely requires judgement today is the prognosis and the frequency and duration estimate. The constraint is not that the information is missing — it is that it is spread across years of notes rather than gathered in one place.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can Stream complete FMLA or disability forms?',
    a: 'Not today. Stream organises the record by medical problem, so a condition’s history is one thread rather than years of dated notes to read through — which is the part that makes these forms slow. Filling structured forms is in development and not available yet, and we would rather say so than imply otherwise.',
  },
];
