<section class="fm">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="fm-wrap">

      <span class="section-label">Guide</span>
      <h1>FMLA paperwork: <em>what makes it slow</em></h1>
      <p class="fm-lede">The form is not hard to understand. It is slow because one field asks you to predict the future, and the only honest basis for that prediction is buried in three years of notes.</p>
      <p class="fm-meta">Written for primary care · Physician-authored · Educational, not legal advice</p>

      <h2>The field that costs the time</h2>
      <p>Most of a certification is transcription. Diagnosis, date of onset, treatment regimen, whether the condition is ongoing — all of it is already in the chart somewhere.</p>
      <p>Then there is the intermittent-leave section, which asks <strong>how often the employee will be unable to work, and for how long each time.</strong></p>
      <p>There is no examination finding that answers that. It is a prediction, and the only defensible basis for it is the patient's own history: how many flares in the past twelve months, how long each one lasted, how many were severe enough to stop them working.</p>

      <div class="fm-note">
        <p>That history exists. It is in the notes from every visit where the condition came up — spread across a year or three, in whatever order the appointments happened. Assembling it takes long enough that the estimate usually gets made from memory instead.</p>
        <p><strong>An estimate made from memory is the one that gets challenged</strong>, and the clinician has nothing to point to when it is.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What the form asks, and where the answer lives</h2>
      <table class="fm-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Where the answer actually is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Diagnosis and onset</td><td>The problem list, and the visit where it was first documented</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Treatment regimen</td><td>The current medication list and the plan from recent visits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Visit frequency</td><td>The encounter history, counted</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Whether it is ongoing</td><td>Clinical judgement, informed by the trajectory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Episode frequency and duration</td><td><strong>Every prior flare, across years of notes</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Prognosis</td><td>Judgement, today</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Five of six are retrieval. The expensive one is the row in bold, because unlike the others it cannot be answered from the most recent note — it needs the whole thread.</p>

      <h2>The categories, and why we are not summarising the thresholds</h2>
      <p>The Department of Labor defines a serious health condition as one involving either inpatient care or continuing treatment by a health care provider, across six categories — including chronic conditions and conditions requiring multiple treatments.</p>
      <p>The specific thresholds attached to those categories — days of incapacity, numbers of visits, the windows they fall in — are set out in the regulation and revised periodically. <strong>We are deliberately not restating them here.</strong> A summary that is one revision out of date, on a page read by a physician completing a legal document, is worse than no summary. Read them at first hand:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/28g-fmla-serious-health-condition" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">DOL Fact Sheet 28G — medical certification</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-V/subchapter-C/part-825" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">29 CFR Part 825 — the FMLA regulations</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Charging for it</h2>
      <p>Certification is paperwork completed for an employer, not a covered medical service, and most practices may charge a form fee. Two things make this smoother: a single written policy rather than a decision per request, and telling the patient the fee when the form arrives rather than after it is done. Check your payer contracts first — a few restrict what can be charged to an enrolled patient.</p>

      <h2>What can be reused, and what has to be verified</h2>
      <div class="fm-box">
        <h3>Safe to carry forward</h3>
        <p>Anything your practice documented: the diagnosis, your visit history, medications you prescribed, episodes recorded at the time they happened. Contemporaneous notes are the strongest evidence available, which is exactly why they are worth assembling rather than approximating.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fm-box">
        <h3>Verify before it goes on the form</h3>
        <p>Anything from an outside source — records from a prior practice, a specialist letter, the patient's own account of episodes you did not see. Knowing where a fact came from is not the same as knowing it is true, and this form carries more weight than most. It may be read by an employer, a third-party administrator, and occasionally a court.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="fm-note">
        <p><strong>Disability paperwork is the same shape.</strong> Short-term, long-term and SSDI forms ask the same underlying questions — what the condition is, what it prevents, and for how long — with every carrier using its own layout. The retrieval problem is identical; only the boxes move.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <div class="fm-faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (<details><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>))}
      </div>

      <div class="fm-fit">
        <h2>Where Stream fits</h2>
        <p>Stream does not complete FMLA or disability forms. That is in development and not available today, and we would rather say so here than let you find out on a call.</p>
        <p>What it changes is the retrieval. Every visit is filed by medical problem rather than by date, so a condition's history is one continuous thread — every flare, every treatment change, every time it was severe enough to matter — instead of three years of notes to read in order. That is the thread the intermittent-leave estimate should be built on.</p>
        <p>Related: <a href="/guides/the-defensible-visit/">The Defensible Visit</a> on documenting medical decision-making so it survives review, and <a href="/guides/school-sports-physicals/">school and sports physicals</a>, which is the same problem in pediatrics.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="fm-disclaim">This guide is educational and reflects general Department of Labor guidance as of October 2026. It is not legal, employment or compliance advice. FMLA regulations and payer policies change — verify against the current regulation and your own contracts before relying on any of it.</p>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Page>
